WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:05.216 --> 00:13.936 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Yellen's
Press Conference March 15,

00:16.456 --> 00:17.716 align:middle
2017 CHAIR YELLEN.

00:17.716 --> 00:19.026 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:19.026 --> 00:21.506 align:middle
Today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:21.546 --> 00:24.586 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate

00:25.026 --> 00:30.316 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point,
bringing it to to 1 percent.

00:31.306 --> 00:34.916 align:middle
Our decision to make another
gradual reduction in the amount

00:34.916 --> 00:39.956 align:middle
of policy accommodation reflects
the economy's continued progress

00:39.956 --> 00:43.726 align:middle
toward the employment and price
stability objectives assigned

00:43.726 --> 00:44.666 align:middle
to us by law.

00:45.416 --> 00:48.636 align:middle
For some time the
Committee has judged that,

00:48.636 --> 00:51.916 align:middle
if economic conditions
evolved as anticipated,

00:52.426 --> 00:56.536 align:middle
gradual increases in the federal
funds rate would likely be

00:56.536 --> 00:59.916 align:middle
appropriate to achieve and
maintain our objectives.

01:00.596 --> 01:03.436 align:middle
Today's decision is
in line with that view

01:03.846 --> 01:06.176 align:middle
and does not represent
a reassessment

01:06.176 --> 01:07.746 align:middle
of the economic outlook

01:08.126 --> 01:11.256 align:middle
or of the appropriate
course for monetary policy.

01:12.156 --> 01:15.116 align:middle
I'll have more to say about
monetary policy shortly,

01:15.536 --> 01:18.456 align:middle
but first I'll review
recent economic developments

01:18.456 --> 01:19.266 align:middle
and the outlook.

01:19.826 --> 01:23.826 align:middle
The economy continues to
expand at a moderate pace.

01:24.366 --> 01:27.466 align:middle
Solid income gains and
relatively high levels

01:27.466 --> 01:31.196 align:middle
of consumer sentiment and
wealth have supported household

01:31.196 --> 01:32.196 align:middle
spending growth.

01:33.066 --> 01:36.916 align:middle
Business investment, which was
soft for much of last year,

01:37.136 --> 01:38.276 align:middle
has firmed somewhat,

01:38.276 --> 01:42.016 align:middle
and business sentiment
is at favorable levels.

01:42.846 --> 01:46.686 align:middle
Overall, we continue to expect
that the economy will expand

01:46.686 --> 01:49.686 align:middle
at a moderate pace over
the next few years.

01:50.416 --> 01:53.636 align:middle
Job gains averaged
about 200,000 per month

01:53.636 --> 01:55.566 align:middle
over the past three months,

01:55.646 --> 01:59.116 align:middle
maintaining the solid pace we
have seen over the past year.

02:00.066 --> 02:02.616 align:middle
The unemployment
rate was 4.7 percent

02:02.616 --> 02:04.816 align:middle
in February, near
its recent low.

02:05.566 --> 02:09.246 align:middle
Broader measures of labor
market underutilization also

02:09.246 --> 02:10.106 align:middle
remain low.

02:11.056 --> 02:14.906 align:middle
Participation in the labor force
has been little changed, on net,

02:15.256 --> 02:16.816 align:middle
for about three years.

02:17.406 --> 02:19.666 align:middle
Given the underlying
downward trend

02:19.666 --> 02:23.116 align:middle
in participation stemming
largely from the aging

02:23.116 --> 02:24.646 align:middle
of the U.S. population,

02:24.646 --> 02:29.036 align:middle
a relatively steady
participation rate is a further

02:29.036 --> 02:31.946 align:middle
sign of improving conditions
in the labor market.

02:33.026 --> 02:34.646 align:middle
Looking ahead, we expect

02:34.646 --> 02:37.376 align:middle
that job conditions will
strengthen somewhat further.

02:38.426 --> 02:41.296 align:middle
Turning to inflation,
the 12-month change

02:41.296 --> 02:46.106 align:middle
in the price index for personal
consumption expenditures rose

02:46.106 --> 02:49.786 align:middle
to nearly 2 percent in
January, up from less

02:49.786 --> 02:51.446 align:middle
than 1 percent last summer.

02:52.456 --> 02:55.726 align:middle
That rise was largely
driven by energy prices,

02:56.156 --> 03:00.886 align:middle
which have been increasing
recently after earlier declines.

03:01.556 --> 03:06.366 align:middle
Core inflation-which excludes
volatile energy and food prices

03:06.786 --> 03:08.936 align:middle
and tends to be a
better indicator

03:08.936 --> 03:11.806 align:middle
of future inflation-has
been little changed

03:11.806 --> 03:14.326 align:middle
in recent months at
about 1 percent.

03:14.326 --> 03:20.246 align:middle
We expect core inflation to
move up and overall inflation

03:20.246 --> 03:24.546 align:middle
to stabilize around 2 percent
over the next couple of years,

03:24.946 --> 03:26.996 align:middle
in line with our
longer-run objective.

03:28.396 --> 03:32.016 align:middle
Let me now turn to the economic
projections that were submitted

03:32.016 --> 03:34.316 align:middle
for this meeting by
Committee participants.

03:34.976 --> 03:38.496 align:middle
As always, participants
conditioned their projections

03:38.836 --> 03:42.846 align:middle
on their own individual views
of appropriate monetary policy,

03:43.526 --> 03:46.926 align:middle
which, in turn, depend on
each participant's assessment

03:47.286 --> 03:49.846 align:middle
of the many factors
that shape the outlook.

03:50.556 --> 03:53.106 align:middle
The median projection for growth

03:53.106 --> 03:57.916 align:middle
of inflation-adjusted gross
domestic product is 2.1 percent

03:57.916 --> 04:02.926 align:middle
this year and next, and edges
down to 1.9 percent in 2019,

04:03.426 --> 04:06.426 align:middle
slightly above its
estimated longer-run rate.

04:07.396 --> 04:10.786 align:middle
The median projection for
the unemployment rate stands

04:10.786 --> 04:15.436 align:middle
at 4.5 percent in the fourth
quarter of this year and remains

04:15.436 --> 04:17.896 align:middle
at that level over
the next two years,

04:18.276 --> 04:20.626 align:middle
modestly below the
median estimate

04:20.626 --> 04:22.476 align:middle
of its longer-run normal rate.

04:23.246 --> 04:28.326 align:middle
Finally, the median inflation
projection is 1.9 percent this

04:28.326 --> 04:33.066 align:middle
year and rises to 2
percent in 2018 and 2019.

04:33.996 --> 04:37.106 align:middle
These economic projections
are very little changed

04:37.106 --> 04:38.886 align:middle
from those made in December.

04:40.016 --> 04:43.326 align:middle
Returning to monetary
policy, the Committee judged

04:43.326 --> 04:44.536 align:middle
that a modest increase

04:44.536 --> 04:47.716 align:middle
in the federal funds rate
is appropriate in light

04:47.776 --> 04:51.576 align:middle
of the economy's solid
progress toward our goals

04:51.576 --> 04:54.156 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

04:55.156 --> 04:56.926 align:middle
Even after this increase,

04:57.186 --> 04:59.836 align:middle
monetary policy remains
accommodative,

05:00.306 --> 05:03.876 align:middle
thus supporting some further
strengthening in the job market

05:04.276 --> 05:07.216 align:middle
and a sustained return
to 2 percent inflation.

05:07.926 --> 05:12.816 align:middle
Today's decision also reflects
our view that waiting too long

05:12.986 --> 05:17.776 align:middle
to scale back some accommodation
could potentially require us

05:17.776 --> 05:22.846 align:middle
to raise rates rapidly sometime
down the road, which, in turn,

05:23.026 --> 05:25.846 align:middle
could risk disrupting
financial markets

05:25.846 --> 05:28.166 align:middle
and pushing the economy
into recession.

05:29.196 --> 05:31.896 align:middle
We continue to expect
that the ongoing strength

05:31.896 --> 05:35.396 align:middle
of the economy will
warrant gradual increases

05:35.396 --> 05:38.036 align:middle
in the federal funds
rate to achieve

05:38.036 --> 05:39.906 align:middle
and maintain our objectives.

05:40.826 --> 05:42.676 align:middle
That's based on our view

05:42.936 --> 05:46.416 align:middle
that the neutral nominal
federal funds rate-that is,

05:46.616 --> 05:49.676 align:middle
the interest rate that is
neither expansionary nor

05:49.676 --> 05:53.086 align:middle
contractionary and keeps
the economy operating

05:53.086 --> 05:56.316 align:middle
on an even keel-is
currently quite low

05:56.316 --> 05:57.936 align:middle
by historical standards.

05:58.496 --> 06:01.856 align:middle
That means that the federal
funds rate does not have to rise

06:01.906 --> 06:05.936 align:middle
by all that much to get to
a neutral policy stance.

06:06.596 --> 06:09.786 align:middle
We also expect the neutral
level of the federal funds rate

06:09.836 --> 06:11.586 align:middle
to rise somewhat over time,

06:11.966 --> 06:15.366 align:middle
meaning that additional
gradual rate hikes are likely

06:15.366 --> 06:18.056 align:middle
to be appropriate over
the next few years

06:18.326 --> 06:20.586 align:middle
to sustain the economic
expansion.

06:21.536 --> 06:24.816 align:middle
Even so, the Committee
continues to anticipate

06:25.036 --> 06:26.866 align:middle
that the longer-run
neutral level

06:26.866 --> 06:29.586 align:middle
of the federal funds
rate is still likely

06:29.586 --> 06:34.046 align:middle
to remain below levels that
prevailed in previous decades.

06:34.736 --> 06:38.236 align:middle
This view is consistent with
participants' projections

06:38.506 --> 06:40.616 align:middle
of appropriate monetary policy.

06:41.356 --> 06:45.356 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate is 1.4

06:45.356 --> 06:49.586 align:middle
percent at the end of this
year, 2.1 percent at the end

06:49.586 --> 06:53.536 align:middle
of next year, and 3
percent at the end of 2019,

06:54.136 --> 06:56.966 align:middle
in line with its
estimated longer-run value.

06:57.656 --> 07:00.536 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in December,

07:00.896 --> 07:03.696 align:middle
the median path for the
federal funds rate is

07:03.696 --> 07:05.146 align:middle
essentially unchanged.

07:05.826 --> 07:09.536 align:middle
As always, the economic
outlook is highly uncertain,

07:09.986 --> 07:12.926 align:middle
and participants will
adjust their assessments

07:12.926 --> 07:16.786 align:middle
of the appropriate path for the
federal funds rate in response

07:16.826 --> 07:20.126 align:middle
to changes to their
economic outlooks and views

07:20.126 --> 07:21.876 align:middle
of the risks to their outlooks.

07:22.566 --> 07:25.756 align:middle
Changes in economic
policies, including fiscal

07:25.756 --> 07:26.946 align:middle
and other policies,

07:27.336 --> 07:29.986 align:middle
could potentially affect
the economic outlook.

07:30.646 --> 07:32.376 align:middle
Of course, it is still too early

07:32.376 --> 07:34.876 align:middle
to know how these
policies will unfold.

07:35.696 --> 07:39.736 align:middle
Moreover, fiscal policy is
only one of many factors

07:40.046 --> 07:41.756 align:middle
that can influence the outlook.

07:42.436 --> 07:46.986 align:middle
In making our decisions, we will
continue-as always-to assess

07:46.986 --> 07:50.706 align:middle
economic conditions
relative to our dual mandate.

07:51.296 --> 07:55.756 align:middle
As I've noted previously, policy
is not on a preset course.

07:56.406 --> 07:59.846 align:middle
Finally, we will continue
to reinvest proceeds

07:59.846 --> 08:02.856 align:middle
from maturing Treasury
securities

08:02.856 --> 08:05.416 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency debt

08:05.716 --> 08:07.666 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities.

08:08.596 --> 08:11.846 align:middle
This policy, by keeping
the Committee's holdings

08:11.846 --> 08:15.596 align:middle
of longer-term securities
at sizable levels,

08:15.596 --> 08:18.016 align:middle
has helped maintain
accommodative

08:18.226 --> 08:19.706 align:middle
financial conditions.

08:20.326 --> 08:23.386 align:middle
As a matter of prudent
planning, we discussed

08:23.386 --> 08:26.516 align:middle
at this meeting a
number of issues related

08:26.516 --> 08:30.076 align:middle
to an eventual change to
our reinvestment policy.

08:30.696 --> 08:34.266 align:middle
We made no decisions, and we
will continue our discussion

08:34.546 --> 08:35.986 align:middle
at subsequent meetings.

08:36.546 --> 08:38.866 align:middle
In keeping with the
principle that the process

08:38.866 --> 08:43.266 align:middle
of normalizing our balance sheet
will be gradual and predictable,

08:43.636 --> 08:46.856 align:middle
we will provide more
information about our plans

08:46.856 --> 08:48.266 align:middle
as it becomes available.

08:48.826 --> 08:49.506 align:middle
Thank you.

08:49.806 --> 08:51.566 align:middle
I'd be happy to take
your questions.

08:52.626 --> 08:53.496 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

08:55.636 --> 08:57.616 align:middle
Thanks very much.

08:57.616 --> 09:01.756 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

09:01.886 --> 09:06.996 align:middle
Could I take you up
on the last topic,

09:06.996 --> 09:11.056 align:middle
which is the balance
sheet normalization?

09:11.056 --> 09:18.796 align:middle
Clearly you said you don't want
to start pulling in the size

09:19.146 --> 09:22.676 align:middle
of the balance sheet

09:22.676 --> 09:25.536 align:middle
until normalization
"is well under way."

09:25.536 --> 09:35.706 align:middle
Could you give us some sort
of sense about what "well

09:35.706 --> 09:39.736 align:middle
under way" means, at
least in your mind?

09:39.736 --> 09:41.726 align:middle
What kind of hurdles
are you setting?

09:41.726 --> 09:46.346 align:middle
What kind of economic conditions
would you like to see?

09:46.346 --> 09:48.336 align:middle
Is it just a matter of the level

09:48.336 --> 09:51.296 align:middle
of the short-term federal funds
rate as being the main issue?

09:51.296 --> 09:54.196 align:middle
And what kind of role
do you see the role

09:54.196 --> 09:56.506 align:middle
of the balance sheet playing

09:56.506 --> 09:58.506 align:middle
in the normalization
process over the longer term?

09:58.506 --> 10:02.066 align:middle
Is it an active tool,
or is it a passive tool?

10:02.066 --> 10:03.246 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

10:03.246 --> 10:06.156 align:middle
So let me start with the
second question first.

10:06.156 --> 10:12.796 align:middle
We've emphasized for quite some
time that the Committee wishes

10:12.796 --> 10:20.666 align:middle
to use variations in the fed
funds rate target-our short-term

10:20.666 --> 10:26.986 align:middle
interest rate target-as our
key active tool of policy.

10:27.076 --> 10:30.506 align:middle
We think it's much
easier in using that tool

10:30.506 --> 10:34.396 align:middle
to communicate the
stance of policy.

10:35.006 --> 10:40.166 align:middle
We have much more experience
with it and have a better idea

10:40.166 --> 10:45.176 align:middle
of its impact on the economy.

10:45.176 --> 10:49.756 align:middle
So while the balance sheet,
asset purchases are a tool

10:49.916 --> 10:57.146 align:middle
that we could conceivably
resort to if we found ourselves

10:57.146 --> 10:59.236 align:middle
in a serious downturn
where we were, again,

10:59.596 --> 11:03.746 align:middle
up against the zero bound and
faced with substantial weakness

11:03.746 --> 11:07.476 align:middle
in the economy, it's not a
tool that we would want to use

11:07.476 --> 11:10.036 align:middle
as a routine tool of policy.

11:10.036 --> 11:13.046 align:middle
You asked what "well
under way" means.

11:13.046 --> 11:16.756 align:middle
I can't give you a
specific answer to that.

11:16.756 --> 11:20.756 align:middle
And I think the right-the
right way to look at it is

11:20.756 --> 11:23.896 align:middle
in qualitative and not
quantitative terms.

11:23.896 --> 11:26.516 align:middle
It doesn't mean some
particular cutoff level

11:26.516 --> 11:32.406 align:middle
for the federal funds rate that,
when we've reached that level,

11:32.406 --> 11:34.546 align:middle
we would consider
ourselves well under way.

11:34.546 --> 11:38.686 align:middle
I think what we want
to have is confidence

11:38.686 --> 11:39.866 align:middle
in the economy's trajectory,

11:39.866 --> 11:42.236 align:middle
a sense that the economy
will make progress,

11:42.236 --> 11:53.146 align:middle
that we're not overly
worried about downside risks

11:53.146 --> 11:56.976 align:middle
and adverse shocks that
could hit the economy

11:57.686 --> 12:01.326 align:middle
that could quickly-after
setting it off on the path

12:01.666 --> 12:05.906 align:middle
to shrinking the balance sheet
gradually over time-cause us

12:06.046 --> 12:09.336 align:middle
to want to begin to add
monetary policy accommodation.

12:09.336 --> 12:14.736 align:middle
So I think it has to do with the
balance of risks and confidence

12:14.736 --> 12:19.076 align:middle
in the economic outlook
and not simply the level

12:19.716 --> 12:21.466 align:middle
of the federal funds rate.

12:21.466 --> 12:22.846 align:middle
JASON LANGE.

12:22.846 --> 12:23.666 align:middle
Hi, thank you.

12:23.666 --> 12:25.486 align:middle
Jason Lange with Reuters.

12:25.486 --> 12:27.836 align:middle
You mentioned that you
don't-that you don't-you want

12:28.086 --> 12:31.556 align:middle
to not have to raise
rates rapidly if you were

12:31.726 --> 12:35.046 align:middle
to fall behind the curve.

12:35.136 --> 12:37.466 align:middle
In the current context,

12:37.606 --> 12:41.166 align:middle
"gradual" has been
very, very gradual.

12:41.336 --> 12:47.186 align:middle
Could you describe what a rapid
rate of increases should be-how

12:47.186 --> 12:48.576 align:middle
that should be understood?

12:48.576 --> 12:49.526 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

12:49.526 --> 12:53.186 align:middle
So I'm not sure that
I can tell you what

12:53.316 --> 12:55.856 align:middle
"a rapid rate of increases" is.

12:55.856 --> 12:58.436 align:middle
I think the trajectory
that you see is the median

12:58.666 --> 13:00.556 align:middle
in our projections,
which, this year,

13:00.556 --> 13:03.146 align:middle
looks to a total
of three increases.

13:03.406 --> 13:06.026 align:middle
That certainly qualifies
as gradual.

13:06.026 --> 13:12.086 align:middle
My comfort in using the term
"gradual" comes back, in part,

13:12.086 --> 13:14.336 align:middle
to my judgment that
the neutral level

13:14.336 --> 13:15.876 align:middle
of the federal funds
rate-namely,

13:16.196 --> 13:18.626 align:middle
the level of the
federal funds rate

13:18.626 --> 13:21.426 align:middle
that would keep the economy
operating on an even keel,

13:21.496 --> 13:23.846 align:middle
that it's a rate where
we neither are pressing

13:24.266 --> 13:29.266 align:middle
on the brake nor pushing down
on the accelerator-that level

13:29.266 --> 13:31.156 align:middle
of interest rates is quite low.

13:31.156 --> 13:35.126 align:middle
So, at present, I see monetary
policy as accommodative-namely,

13:35.126 --> 13:38.866 align:middle
the current level of the
federal funds rate is below

13:38.866 --> 13:41.926 align:middle
that neutral rate, but not very
far below the neutral rate.

13:41.926 --> 13:44.796 align:middle
We're closing in, I think,
on our employment objective.

13:44.796 --> 13:50.766 align:middle
We're coming closer on
our inflation objective.

13:50.766 --> 13:55.346 align:middle
As we reach those objectives,
and particularly in light

13:55.346 --> 13:58.636 align:middle
of the fact that we see
the risks to the outlook

13:58.636 --> 14:02.526 align:middle
as roughly balanced at this
point-and that's been our

14:02.526 --> 14:05.706 align:middle
assessment for the last
several meetings-it looks to us

14:05.706 --> 14:11.926 align:middle
to be appropriate to gradually
raise the federal funds rate

14:11.976 --> 14:14.496 align:middle
back in the direction
of neutral.

14:14.496 --> 14:19.246 align:middle
And exactly how many
increases is that?

14:19.246 --> 14:22.646 align:middle
You know, the SEP
gives you a sense

14:22.836 --> 14:28.876 align:middle
of what Committee participants
envision in a concrete sense.

14:29.186 --> 14:31.246 align:middle
But, you know, if it's
one more or one less,

14:31.356 --> 14:35.786 align:middle
I think that still-that still
qualifies to my mind as gradual,

14:35.786 --> 14:42.426 align:middle
I think, if you compare it with
any previous tightening cycle.

14:42.426 --> 14:44.316 align:middle
I remember when rates
were raised

14:44.626 --> 14:47.606 align:middle
at every meeting
starting in mid-2004.

14:47.606 --> 14:49.756 align:middle
And I think people thought

14:49.756 --> 14:51.806 align:middle
that was a gradual
pace, a measured pace.

14:51.806 --> 14:55.656 align:middle
And we're certainly not
envisioning something like that.

14:55.656 --> 14:56.576 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:56.576 --> 14:57.716 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

14:57.716 --> 15:01.246 align:middle
Both the OECD and the IMF
have raised their forecasts,

15:01.246 --> 15:05.006 align:middle
in part because of-for
the U.S. growth-in part

15:05.006 --> 15:07.016 align:middle
because of the policies expected

15:07.016 --> 15:11.456 align:middle
from the new Administration,
yet the Fed has not.

15:11.456 --> 15:15.776 align:middle
And I take it from your
comment at the very beginning

15:15.776 --> 15:19.286 align:middle
that this-these forecasts today
represent no reassessment.

15:19.286 --> 15:23.336 align:middle
Has the Committee discussed
what policy might look

15:23.336 --> 15:25.856 align:middle
like in the event that there
are large tax cuts passed

15:25.856 --> 15:28.036 align:middle
or infrastructure
spending passed?

15:28.036 --> 15:32.506 align:middle
And what might policy look like
if those policies become law?

15:32.766 --> 15:35.956 align:middle
Finally, why did you remove
the word "only" before the word

15:36.176 --> 15:40.106 align:middle
"gradual" when you talked
about future rate increases?

15:40.466 --> 15:41.336 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

15:41.336 --> 15:45.546 align:middle
So we have not discussed in
detail potential policy changes

15:45.546 --> 15:49.786 align:middle
that could be put into place,
and we have not tried to map

15:49.926 --> 15:53.096 align:middle
out what our response would be
to particular policy measures.

15:53.096 --> 15:55.106 align:middle
We recognize that there is great
uncertainty about the timing,

15:55.106 --> 15:58.156 align:middle
the size, the character of
policy changes that may be put

15:58.156 --> 16:01.796 align:middle
in place and don't think
that that's a decision,

16:02.146 --> 16:07.786 align:middle
or a set of decisions, that we
need to make until we know more

16:08.096 --> 16:11.226 align:middle
about what policy changes
will go into effect.

16:11.226 --> 16:13.626 align:middle
So I do want to emphasize that,

16:13.626 --> 16:15.676 align:middle
while some participants
have penciled

16:15.676 --> 16:18.746 align:middle
in some fiscal policy changes
into their projections,

16:18.746 --> 16:22.516 align:middle
that the basis for today's
decision is simply our

16:22.516 --> 16:24.236 align:middle
assessment of the
progress of the economy

16:24.236 --> 16:25.986 align:middle
against our long-established
goals

16:25.986 --> 16:28.906 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

16:29.226 --> 16:32.546 align:middle
There's nothing that we
have done or anticipate

16:32.546 --> 16:33.406 align:middle
that is a speculation.

16:33.406 --> 16:34.656 align:middle
I think it's fair to say,

16:34.656 --> 16:37.186 align:middle
there's nothing that's
a speculation

16:37.186 --> 16:39.946 align:middle
about preemptive responses
to future policy moves.

16:40.776 --> 16:43.686 align:middle
We have plenty of time
to see what happens.

16:45.016 --> 16:47.446 align:middle
We did remove the word "only"

16:47.956 --> 16:50.666 align:middle
in the statement
today from "gradual."

16:51.006 --> 16:54.726 align:middle
I think this is something that
shouldn't be overinterpreted.

16:54.726 --> 16:57.986 align:middle
I regard it as a
relatively small change

16:58.236 --> 17:01.156 align:middle
and think it's appropriate
for you to consider it

17:01.156 --> 17:03.276 align:middle
in the context, for example,

17:03.276 --> 17:07.346 align:middle
of the fact that our economic
projections are virtually

17:07.346 --> 17:11.446 align:middle
identical to those that
we issued in December.

17:11.726 --> 17:15.436 align:middle
They're essentially unchanged
both in terms of the path

17:15.436 --> 17:19.046 align:middle
of the economy and the path
of the federal funds rate.

17:19.046 --> 17:25.526 align:middle
So we have carried out
a modest adjustment

17:25.766 --> 17:27.516 align:middle
of the federal funds rate

17:27.586 --> 17:31.696 align:middle
because we've seen the
economy progressing

17:31.866 --> 17:33.946 align:middle
over the last several months

17:33.946 --> 17:36.736 align:middle
in exactly the way
that we anticipated.

17:37.146 --> 17:39.676 align:middle
We haven't in any
way changed our view

17:39.996 --> 17:43.696 align:middle
about where the economy
is heading or the risks.

17:43.826 --> 17:48.206 align:middle
We had long said that if the
economy progressed, and-it's,

17:48.266 --> 17:50.096 align:middle
you know, it's been
doing nicely, I think,

17:50.096 --> 17:54.516 align:middle
in making progress, in showing
resilience, and we have,

17:54.516 --> 17:58.486 align:middle
you know, have some confidence
in the path the economy is on.

17:58.736 --> 18:04.306 align:middle
And if we continue to feel
that, we will likely regard it

18:04.306 --> 18:09.006 align:middle
as appropriate to make
some further moves

18:09.046 --> 18:13.396 align:middle
to scale back accommodation
to move toward neutral,

18:13.606 --> 18:15.716 align:middle
along the lines in the SEP.

18:15.716 --> 18:18.236 align:middle
Now, obviously, there
are surprises.

18:18.236 --> 18:20.576 align:middle
Our economic forecasts
can change.

18:20.876 --> 18:23.186 align:middle
But the word "gradual," I think,

18:23.506 --> 18:26.876 align:middle
emphasizes that if things
continue in the manner

18:26.876 --> 18:29.366 align:middle
that we've been going,
as we have said now

18:29.366 --> 18:31.186 align:middle
for quite some time, we think

18:31.186 --> 18:33.686 align:middle
that gradual-some
gradual increases

18:33.686 --> 18:36.136 align:middle
in the federal funds
rate-will be appropriate.

18:36.136 --> 18:37.536 align:middle
And this is not a, you know,

18:37.706 --> 18:41.636 align:middle
this is not a significant-this
is not a significant change.

18:45.476 --> 18:46.496 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

18:46.496 --> 18:47.726 align:middle
Peter Barnes, Fox
Business, ma'am.

18:47.856 --> 18:50.226 align:middle
Speaking of fiscal policy,
have you had a chance to meet

18:50.226 --> 18:53.446 align:middle
with the new Treasury
Secretary yet, Mr. Mnuchin?

18:53.446 --> 18:55.446 align:middle
If not, when will
you meet with him?

18:55.866 --> 18:57.056 align:middle
What do you want to
talk to him about?

18:57.426 --> 18:58.966 align:middle
And, separately, have
you had a chance to talk

18:58.966 --> 19:01.676 align:middle
to President Trump
yet or meet with him?

19:01.816 --> 19:05.616 align:middle
And, if not, would you
like to, and would you,

19:05.616 --> 19:06.396 align:middle
and what would you talk about?

19:06.566 --> 19:06.846 align:middle
Thank you.

19:07.416 --> 19:07.896 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

19:07.896 --> 19:11.056 align:middle
I've met a couple of times
with the Treasury Secretary,

19:11.056 --> 19:12.586 align:middle
and I'm getting to know him.

19:12.946 --> 19:16.736 align:middle
And I think, you know, it's
traditional for Fed Chairs

19:16.736 --> 19:20.306 align:middle
and Treasury Secretaries
to meet on a regular basis,

19:20.306 --> 19:25.376 align:middle
and I fully expect to
have a strong relationship

19:25.376 --> 19:26.776 align:middle
with Secretary Mnuchin.

19:26.776 --> 19:30.556 align:middle
We have had very good
discussions about the economy,

19:30.556 --> 19:34.676 align:middle
about regulatory objectives,
the work of the FSOC,

19:34.966 --> 19:38.776 align:middle
global economic developments,
and I look forward

19:38.776 --> 19:40.826 align:middle
to continuing to work with him.

19:41.106 --> 19:43.786 align:middle
I was introduced
to the President.

19:43.786 --> 19:47.106 align:middle
I had a very brief meeting
and appreciated that as well.

19:47.106 --> 19:49.696 align:middle
ANA SWANSON.

19:49.696 --> 19:51.456 align:middle
Ana Swanson, the
Washington Post.

19:51.756 --> 19:53.266 align:middle
You said that the neutral level

19:53.266 --> 19:54.806 align:middle
of the federal funds
rate is quite low.

19:54.806 --> 19:58.016 align:middle
How close do you judge it
to be to the inflation rate?

19:58.016 --> 20:00.906 align:middle
And what do you anticipate
will be the force pushing

20:00.906 --> 20:03.476 align:middle
up the neutral interest rate
over the next few years?

20:03.866 --> 20:05.436 align:middle
Could fiscal policy
be among those?

20:05.906 --> 20:06.946 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

20:06.946 --> 20:11.066 align:middle
So I've given a number of
recent speeches on this topic

20:11.106 --> 20:14.906 align:middle
where I've developed
my views more fully.

20:15.436 --> 20:20.916 align:middle
I would say, over the longer
run-that means going several

20:20.916 --> 20:24.356 align:middle
years out-I think
the evidence suggests

20:24.486 --> 20:28.296 align:middle
that the neutral rate may
be something, in real terms,

20:28.766 --> 20:32.226 align:middle
that might be close to 1 percent
or a little bit under that.

20:32.596 --> 20:36.516 align:middle
That would be consistent with
the median longer-run value

20:36.826 --> 20:41.386 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
in our economic projections

20:41.386 --> 20:43.896 align:middle
for the last several meetings.

20:44.166 --> 20:49.146 align:middle
Three percent is the longer-run
normal federal funds rate

20:49.186 --> 20:51.376 align:middle
that participants estimate.

20:51.616 --> 20:55.946 align:middle
In real terms, with a 2 percent
inflation objective-that's 1

20:55.946 --> 20:57.736 align:middle
percent in real terms.

20:58.106 --> 21:01.926 align:middle
And I've indicated-why
is it so low?

21:02.306 --> 21:05.236 align:middle
Well, I think there's very
strong evidence that's

21:05.236 --> 21:08.986 align:middle
accumulated that this rate
has been falling not just

21:08.986 --> 21:12.556 align:middle
in the United States, but
in many advanced nations,

21:12.816 --> 21:16.936 align:middle
and the decline probably
predates the financial crisis.

21:17.246 --> 21:20.876 align:middle
I think in part it reflects
slowing population growth

21:20.876 --> 21:24.116 align:middle
and also slow productivity
growth here

21:24.116 --> 21:26.716 align:middle
and in many other
advanced nations.

21:26.716 --> 21:32.686 align:middle
But some recent work suggests
that, at the present time,

21:32.926 --> 21:35.696 align:middle
the neutral real rate
is yet lower than that,

21:35.886 --> 21:41.376 align:middle
and some estimates place it
around zero in real terms.

21:41.376 --> 21:49.146 align:middle
So I think the lower current
rate arguably reflects headwinds

21:49.206 --> 21:52.576 align:middle
that are left over from
the financial crisis.

21:52.636 --> 22:01.456 align:middle
One form of headwind, I think,
has been caution and restraint

22:01.456 --> 22:04.336 align:middle
and risk aversion on
the part of households

22:04.336 --> 22:07.996 align:middle
and businesses that's held
back spending decisions.

22:07.996 --> 22:10.396 align:middle
And, I suppose, my
judgment that it'll move

22:10.396 --> 22:14.116 align:middle
up over time reflects
a notion that part

22:14.116 --> 22:17.236 align:middle
of that will gradually
dissipate over the years.

22:17.236 --> 22:21.676 align:middle
So that's a sense of where I
think-now, there is uncertainty

22:21.676 --> 22:22.876 align:middle
about the neutral rate.

22:23.176 --> 22:27.266 align:middle
And, as you mentioned,
it is-it can be affected

22:27.266 --> 22:29.506 align:middle
by shifts in fiscal policy.

22:29.776 --> 22:32.496 align:middle
How the neutral rate is affected

22:32.496 --> 22:35.886 align:middle
by fiscal policy-that
really depends importantly

22:35.886 --> 22:39.946 align:middle
on the nature, the size
of the fiscal shift

22:39.946 --> 22:42.426 align:middle
and the effect it
has both on demand

22:42.426 --> 22:44.036 align:middle
and supply in the economy.

22:45.166 --> 22:46.526 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

22:48.236 --> 22:49.766 align:middle
Thank you.

22:49.836 --> 22:51.506 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

22:52.406 --> 22:54.446 align:middle
Chair Yellen, between the
release of the minutes

22:54.446 --> 22:57.066 align:middle
of the previous meeting late
last month and your speech

22:57.066 --> 23:01.106 align:middle
in Chicago earlier this
month, market expectations

23:01.106 --> 23:04.306 align:middle
about an increase in rates today
changed quite dramatically.

23:04.676 --> 23:06.816 align:middle
What happened over the
course of those two weeks

23:06.816 --> 23:11.386 align:middle
to make officials far more
interested in signaling the idea

23:11.386 --> 23:13.356 align:middle
of raising rates
at today's meeting?

23:13.856 --> 23:17.826 align:middle
And why do you think the
market was so out of sync

23:17.876 --> 23:19.226 align:middle
with where the central bank was?

23:19.906 --> 23:21.336 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

23:21.336 --> 23:26.446 align:middle
So, when I look at our
sequence of communications,

23:27.166 --> 23:30.796 align:middle
they seem to me to have
been reasonably consistent

23:31.306 --> 23:33.446 align:middle
over this entire period.

23:33.726 --> 23:39.486 align:middle
We had indicated in December
that we expect-we saw the risks

23:39.486 --> 23:43.346 align:middle
as balanced, and if
the economy continued

23:43.346 --> 23:46.336 align:middle
to progress along the
lines we expected,

23:46.596 --> 23:49.886 align:middle
that several rate increases
would likely be appropriate.

23:50.486 --> 23:53.906 align:middle
The minutes of our
January meeting indicated

23:53.906 --> 23:58.446 align:middle
that many participants
thought that an increase

23:58.446 --> 24:02.096 align:middle
in the funds rate would
be appropriate fairly soon

24:02.406 --> 24:05.076 align:middle
if things continued
along those lines.

24:05.546 --> 24:11.606 align:middle
I indicated in my congressional
testimony that I thought that,

24:11.886 --> 24:16.636 align:middle
indeed, the economy
was progressing in line

24:16.636 --> 24:18.106 align:middle
with our expectations.

24:18.596 --> 24:26.516 align:middle
And, as I think all of us-having
that expectation and that

24:26.516 --> 24:31.366 align:middle
if the economy continued
to progress along the lines

24:31.366 --> 24:34.976 align:middle
that we expected and we
continued to see the risks

24:35.216 --> 24:39.866 align:middle
as balanced-do regard
it as appropriate

24:39.906 --> 24:44.266 align:middle
to gradually remove
accommodation that's in place

24:44.586 --> 24:50.696 align:middle
by having several interest
rate increases this year.

24:51.756 --> 24:55.626 align:middle
As we saw the data
continue to come in in line

24:55.626 --> 25:00.316 align:middle
with our expectations, my
colleagues and I spoke out

25:00.316 --> 25:04.166 align:middle
and indicated that, indeed,
that had been and continued

25:04.596 --> 25:06.256 align:middle
to be our expectations.

25:06.646 --> 25:10.176 align:middle
Now, you know, when you ask
me, how did we get out of sync

25:10.176 --> 25:14.266 align:middle
with the market, this is
something I tried to reflect

25:14.266 --> 25:17.126 align:middle
on a bit in the remarks
I made in Chicago.

25:17.476 --> 25:23.076 align:middle
And, of course, it is true
that in 2015 and in 2016 each,

25:23.506 --> 25:26.276 align:middle
we raised the federal
funds rate only once,

25:26.746 --> 25:31.026 align:middle
and perhaps market participants
have been influenced

25:31.126 --> 25:32.736 align:middle
by that pattern.

25:33.066 --> 25:37.676 align:middle
I did try to explain the
reasons why we had moved

25:37.676 --> 25:40.626 align:middle
so slowly during
those two years.

25:40.626 --> 25:46.586 align:middle
And it reflects, I think, a
set of shocks partly emanating

25:46.586 --> 25:51.426 align:middle
from the global economy and
risks that we saw to the outlook

25:51.696 --> 25:55.796 align:middle
as well as more fundamental
assessments-reassessments

25:56.226 --> 26:00.206 align:middle
pertaining to the neutral
level of the federal funds rate

26:00.516 --> 26:03.936 align:middle
and the longer-run normal
level of the unemployment rate.

26:03.936 --> 26:07.186 align:middle
So I think there are
reasons, but it is important

26:07.466 --> 26:12.556 align:middle
for the public to understand
that we're getting closer

26:12.556 --> 26:16.806 align:middle
to reaching our objectives;
that policy is accommodative;

26:17.116 --> 26:18.566 align:middle
that although the level

26:18.566 --> 26:22.686 align:middle
of the neutral federal funds
rate is probably quite low,

26:22.926 --> 26:27.946 align:middle
we nevertheless have an
accommodative stance of policy;

26:28.156 --> 26:29.766 align:middle
and it will be appropriate

26:29.766 --> 26:33.006 align:middle
to gradually move
toward a neutral stance

26:33.766 --> 26:36.636 align:middle
if we continue on
the path we're on.

26:37.886 --> 26:39.136 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

26:39.436 --> 26:42.206 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

26:42.236 --> 26:45.406 align:middle
The Bank for International
Settlements has raised concerns

26:45.406 --> 26:47.686 align:middle
that central banks are being
insufficiently attentive

26:47.716 --> 26:50.336 align:middle
to asset price-excuse me,
asset price inflation.

26:50.976 --> 26:52.536 align:middle
And stock market investors

26:52.536 --> 26:54.686 align:middle
in the United States certainly
don't seem to be waiting

26:54.686 --> 26:55.776 align:middle
for the Trump Administration

26:55.776 --> 26:57.676 align:middle
to actually implement
its fiscal policies.

26:58.166 --> 26:59.946 align:middle
And I guess I'm just
curious to know how much

26:59.976 --> 27:01.546 align:middle
of a concern that is for you.

27:01.966 --> 27:05.256 align:middle
And, if not, why not, given
the remarkably elevated level

27:05.256 --> 27:06.816 align:middle
of stock price evaluations?

27:07.226 --> 27:07.726 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

27:07.726 --> 27:10.316 align:middle
Well, we do look at
financial conditions

27:12.346 --> 27:16.406 align:middle
in formulating our
view of the outlook,

27:16.866 --> 27:22.696 align:middle
and stock prices do figure
into financial conditions.

27:22.696 --> 27:27.776 align:middle
So I think the higher level
of stock prices is one factor

27:28.046 --> 27:29.576 align:middle
that looks like it's likely

27:29.656 --> 27:32.606 align:middle
to somewhat boost
consumption spending.

27:33.566 --> 27:37.716 align:middle
We also noticed that, in
the last several months,

27:37.886 --> 27:40.396 align:middle
that risk spreads, particularly

27:40.536 --> 27:44.266 align:middle
for lower-grade corporate
issuers, have narrowed,

27:44.266 --> 27:46.696 align:middle
which is another signal

27:46.696 --> 27:51.496 align:middle
that financial conditions
have become somewhat easier.

27:51.846 --> 27:55.416 align:middle
Now, on the other side,
longer-term interest rates are

27:55.416 --> 27:59.156 align:middle
up some in recent months, and
the dollar is a little stronger.

27:59.686 --> 28:01.416 align:middle
How does that net out?

28:01.586 --> 28:04.066 align:middle
There are private-sector
analysts

28:04.116 --> 28:09.826 align:middle
that produce financial
conditions indices that attempt

28:09.866 --> 28:13.206 align:middle
to aggregate all these
different factors affecting

28:13.206 --> 28:14.666 align:middle
financial conditions.

28:15.016 --> 28:22.276 align:middle
And for some of the more
prominent analysts and indices,

28:22.626 --> 28:24.916 align:middle
I think the conclusion
they have reached is

28:24.976 --> 28:28.106 align:middle
that financial conditions,
on balance, have eased,

28:28.406 --> 28:30.756 align:middle
and that's partly driven
by the stock market.

28:30.826 --> 28:33.706 align:middle
So that is a factor that
affects the outlook.

28:34.126 --> 28:36.796 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

28:36.846 --> 28:39.326 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

28:39.326 --> 28:41.906 align:middle
You and Secretary
Mnuchin will be meeting

28:41.906 --> 28:44.906 align:middle
with your G-20 colleagues
in Germany this weekend.

28:45.166 --> 28:47.016 align:middle
What do you expect
to find there?

28:47.016 --> 28:49.646 align:middle
Do you think the group
assessment is going to be

28:49.646 --> 28:51.566 align:middle
that the world economy
is finally

28:51.566 --> 28:53.056 align:middle
out of the woods, doing better?

28:53.346 --> 28:54.646 align:middle
Or do you think that
there's still going

28:54.646 --> 28:56.086 align:middle
to be worries about risk?

28:56.466 --> 28:59.016 align:middle
And would one of
those risk worries be

28:59.196 --> 29:01.066 align:middle
that the Fed might
raise rates too quickly?

29:01.066 --> 29:01.826 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

29:02.616 --> 29:05.826 align:middle
Well, we always exchange
views on the economic outlook

29:05.826 --> 29:10.216 align:middle
and developments in our country,
and it will be my objective

29:10.306 --> 29:16.096 align:middle
to explain-explain U.S.
monetary policy and to try

29:16.096 --> 29:18.216 align:middle
to make the same points to them

29:18.216 --> 29:23.636 align:middle
that I've made here already
today about what the outlook is

29:23.636 --> 29:27.376 align:middle
for monetary policy
in the United States.

29:27.466 --> 29:30.426 align:middle
I think it's fair to say

29:30.426 --> 29:33.486 align:middle
that the global economy
is doing better.

29:33.486 --> 29:36.866 align:middle
It's growing a bit more
strongly than it was, perhaps,

29:36.866 --> 29:41.246 align:middle
the last time I got together
that the-with my counterparts

29:41.246 --> 29:45.456 align:middle
in the G-20-that the risks do
look somewhat more balanced.

29:45.736 --> 29:50.626 align:middle
But there remain a set of very
significant risks, medium term,

29:50.626 --> 29:53.196 align:middle
facing the global
economy, and I'm sure

29:53.196 --> 29:55.086 align:middle
that those will be
discussed as well.

29:55.646 --> 29:59.306 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

29:59.786 --> 30:02.036 align:middle
Hi, Jim Puzzanghera with
the Los Angeles Times.

30:02.036 --> 30:04.076 align:middle
You said you and your colleagues
aren't making assumptions

30:04.076 --> 30:05.896 align:middle
about stimulative
fiscal policies,

30:06.326 --> 30:07.646 align:middle
but many other people are.

30:07.646 --> 30:10.286 align:middle
Business and consumer confidence
has jumped since the election.

30:10.636 --> 30:14.066 align:middle
Homebuilder sentiment today was
at its highest level since 2005.

30:14.466 --> 30:17.266 align:middle
Are you concerned about the
effects on the economy if some

30:17.266 --> 30:19.696 align:middle
of these policies,
such as tax cuts

30:19.696 --> 30:20.916 align:middle
and infrastructure spending,

30:21.296 --> 30:23.266 align:middle
don't get enacted
or are delayed?

30:23.266 --> 30:23.506 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

30:24.836 --> 30:29.706 align:middle
So we recognize-our statement
actually last time noted

30:29.976 --> 30:31.796 align:middle
that there had been
an improvement,

30:31.796 --> 30:35.256 align:middle
a marked improvement in business
and household sentiment.

30:37.236 --> 30:42.496 align:middle
It's uncertain just how much
sentiment actually impacts

30:42.496 --> 30:44.246 align:middle
spending decisions.

30:44.246 --> 30:48.836 align:middle
And I wouldn't say at this point
that I have seen hard evidence

30:49.496 --> 30:53.136 align:middle
of any change in
spending decisions based

30:53.136 --> 30:55.406 align:middle
on expectations about
the future.

30:55.786 --> 31:02.626 align:middle
We exchange around the
table what we learn

31:02.676 --> 31:06.206 align:middle
from our many business contacts,
and I think it's fair to say

31:06.586 --> 31:07.966 align:middle
that many of my colleagues

31:07.966 --> 31:11.246 align:middle
and I note a much
more optimistic frame

31:11.246 --> 31:15.416 align:middle
of mind among many-many
businesses in recent months.

31:15.476 --> 31:17.886 align:middle
But, I'd say, most of
the business people

31:17.886 --> 31:22.176 align:middle
that we've talked to also
have a wait-and-see attitude

31:22.396 --> 31:25.866 align:middle
and are very hopeful
that they will be able

31:25.866 --> 31:30.056 align:middle
to expand investment and are
looking forward to doing that

31:30.306 --> 31:32.136 align:middle
but are waiting to
see what will happen.

31:32.136 --> 31:34.196 align:middle
So we will watch that.

31:34.196 --> 31:37.636 align:middle
And, of course, if we
were to see a major shift

31:37.636 --> 31:40.966 align:middle
in spending reflecting
those expectations,

31:41.016 --> 31:42.876 align:middle
that could very well
affect the outlook.

31:42.876 --> 31:46.586 align:middle
I'm not seeing it-I'm not
seeing that at this point,

31:46.666 --> 31:50.566 align:middle
but the shift in sentiment
is obvious and notable.

31:51.126 --> 31:52.356 align:middle
KATE DAVIDSON.

31:52.776 --> 31:56.366 align:middle
Thank you, Madam Chair.

31:56.396 --> 31:58.476 align:middle
Kate Davidson from Dow Jones.

31:58.596 --> 32:01.966 align:middle
There's a perception out there
that the Fed could somehow stand

32:01.966 --> 32:05.266 align:middle
in the way of some of the
economic growth policies

32:05.266 --> 32:07.376 align:middle
that the new Administration
is pursuing.

32:07.376 --> 32:11.316 align:middle
Given that the Fed is
projecting 1.8 percent growth

32:11.316 --> 32:13.836 align:middle
in the long run, is this a
potential point of conflict

32:13.916 --> 32:16.026 align:middle
for the Fed and the
new Administration?

32:17.096 --> 32:17.726 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

32:17.726 --> 32:20.396 align:middle
So I don't believe it
is a point of conflict.

32:20.396 --> 32:24.806 align:middle
We would certainly welcome
stronger economic growth

32:24.806 --> 32:27.596 align:middle
in a context of price stability.

32:27.906 --> 32:33.186 align:middle
And if policies were put
in place to speed growth

32:33.286 --> 32:38.516 align:middle
that I've certainly urged
Congress and the Administration

32:38.516 --> 32:42.346 align:middle
to consider-policies that
would boost productivity growth

32:42.346 --> 32:46.436 align:middle
and raise the economy's
so-called speed limit

32:46.436 --> 32:49.316 align:middle
or potential to grow-I think

32:49.316 --> 32:52.886 align:middle
that those would be-those
would be very welcome changes

32:53.196 --> 32:54.506 align:middle
that we would like to see.

32:55.926 --> 32:57.916 align:middle
KATHLEEN HAYS.

32:57.976 --> 32:59.536 align:middle
Chair Yellen, Kathleen Hays.

32:59.536 --> 33:01.266 align:middle
Oh, excuse me, Kathleen
Hays from Bloomberg.

33:01.266 --> 33:03.266 align:middle
I'm going to try to
take the opposite side

33:03.266 --> 33:06.226 align:middle
of this because-on this question
about market expectations

33:06.226 --> 33:08.496 align:middle
and how the markets
got things wrong,

33:08.496 --> 33:11.666 align:middle
and then how you say the Fed
suddenly clarified what it

33:11.666 --> 33:12.456 align:middle
already said.

33:12.816 --> 33:14.566 align:middle
But, for example,
if the-if you look

33:14.566 --> 33:18.196 align:middle
at the Atlanta Fed's latest GDP
tracker for the first quarter,

33:18.456 --> 33:19.926 align:middle
it's down to 0.9 percent.

33:20.626 --> 33:22.826 align:middle
We had a retail sales
report that was mixed.

33:22.826 --> 33:25.046 align:middle
Granted the, you
know, upward revisions

33:25.046 --> 33:26.946 align:middle
of previous months
make it look better,

33:26.946 --> 33:28.606 align:middle
but the consumer does
not appear to be roaring

33:28.606 --> 33:29.416 align:middle
in the first quarter, kind

33:29.416 --> 33:32.586 align:middle
of underscoring the wait-and-see
attitude you just mentioned.

33:33.366 --> 33:35.646 align:middle
If you look at measures
of labor compensation,

33:35.646 --> 33:37.946 align:middle
you note in the statement
that they're not moving up,

33:38.406 --> 33:40.436 align:middle
and, in fact, they are.

33:40.436 --> 33:41.516 align:middle
And if you look at
average-there are

33:41.516 --> 33:42.536 align:middle
so many things you can look at.

33:42.536 --> 33:46.506 align:middle
And you yourself have said
in the past that the fact

33:46.606 --> 33:49.206 align:middle
that that is happening is
perhaps an indication there's

33:49.206 --> 33:50.336 align:middle
still slack in the labor market.

33:50.336 --> 33:54.956 align:middle
I guess my question is this:
In another sense, what happened

33:54.956 --> 33:56.236 align:middle
between December and March?

33:56.236 --> 33:57.496 align:middle
GDP is tracking very low.

33:57.806 --> 33:59.856 align:middle
Measures of labor
compensation are not threatening

33:59.856 --> 34:01.696 align:middle
to boost inflation
any time fast.

34:02.066 --> 34:04.166 align:middle
The consumer is not
picking up very much.

34:04.266 --> 34:05.626 align:middle
Fiscal policy-we don't
know what's going

34:05.626 --> 34:07.026 align:middle
to happen with Donald Trump.

34:07.026 --> 34:09.646 align:middle
And yet you have
to raise rates now.

34:09.646 --> 34:12.356 align:middle
So what is the-what is
the motivation here?

34:12.586 --> 34:15.426 align:middle
The economy is so far from
your forecast in terms of GDP,

34:15.606 --> 34:17.126 align:middle
why does the Fed
have to move now?

34:17.126 --> 34:18.966 align:middle
What does this signal, then,
about the rest of the year?

34:19.536 --> 34:20.366 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

34:20.366 --> 34:22.726 align:middle
So GDP is a pretty
noisy indicator.

34:22.726 --> 34:26.946 align:middle
If one averages through
several quarters,

34:27.306 --> 34:31.146 align:middle
I would describe our economy
as one that has been growing

34:31.146 --> 34:33.456 align:middle
around 2 percent per year.

34:33.766 --> 34:36.466 align:middle
And as you can see
from our projections,

34:36.466 --> 34:40.066 align:middle
we-that's something
we expect to continue

34:40.326 --> 34:42.246 align:middle
over the next couple of years.

34:42.646 --> 34:50.316 align:middle
Now, that pace of growth has
been consistent with a pace

34:50.316 --> 34:56.246 align:middle
of job creation that is more
rapid than what is sustainable

34:56.456 --> 35:01.816 align:middle
if labor force participation
begins to move down in line

35:02.066 --> 35:04.856 align:middle
with what we see as
its longer-run trend

35:05.046 --> 35:06.726 align:middle
with an aging population.

35:07.346 --> 35:10.796 align:middle
Now, unemployment
hasn't moved that much,

35:10.796 --> 35:14.656 align:middle
in part because people have
been drawn into the labor force.

35:14.756 --> 35:18.426 align:middle
Labor force participation,
as I mentioned in my remarks,

35:18.426 --> 35:21.576 align:middle
has been about flat over
the last three years.

35:21.576 --> 35:25.006 align:middle
So, in that sense,
the economy has shown

35:25.276 --> 35:29.946 align:middle
over the last several years that
it may have had more room to run

35:30.156 --> 35:34.476 align:middle
than some people might have
estimated, and that's been good.

35:34.476 --> 35:36.606 align:middle
It's meant we've
had a great deal

35:36.606 --> 35:40.036 align:middle
of job creation over
these years.

35:40.386 --> 35:43.466 align:middle
And there could be-there
could be room left

35:43.466 --> 35:46.086 align:middle
for that to play out further.

35:46.086 --> 35:49.116 align:middle
In fact, look, policy
remains accommodative.

35:49.386 --> 35:52.616 align:middle
We expect further improvement
in the labor market.

35:52.726 --> 35:56.806 align:middle
We expect the unemployment rate
to move down further and to stay

35:56.806 --> 35:59.136 align:middle
down for the next several years.

35:59.176 --> 36:02.096 align:middle
So we do expect that the path

36:02.096 --> 36:05.416 align:middle
of policy we think is
appropriate is one that is going

36:05.416 --> 36:07.466 align:middle
to lead to some further
strengthening

36:07.676 --> 36:08.646 align:middle
in the labor market.

36:09.556 --> 36:10.726 align:middle
KATHLEEN HAYS.

36:11.176 --> 36:16.026 align:middle
[Inaudible] Just quickly, then,
I just want to underscore-I want

36:16.176 --> 36:18.846 align:middle
to ask you, so following on
that, you expect it to move.

36:18.846 --> 36:19.576 align:middle
But what if it doesn't?

36:19.576 --> 36:20.906 align:middle
What if GDP doesn't pick up?

36:20.986 --> 36:22.906 align:middle
What if you don't see
wage measures rising?

36:22.906 --> 36:24.036 align:middle
What if you don't-what

36:24.036 --> 36:26.466 align:middle
if the core PCE gets
stuck at 1.7 percent?

36:26.666 --> 36:28.236 align:middle
Would you-is it your
view, perhaps,

36:28.426 --> 36:32.366 align:middle
that if there's a risk right now
in the median forecast for dots,

36:32.366 --> 36:33.776 align:middle
that it's fewer hikes this year,

36:33.776 --> 36:35.916 align:middle
rather than the consensus,
or more?

36:36.116 --> 36:36.356 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:36.636 --> 36:40.336 align:middle
Well, look, our policy
is not set in stone.

36:40.556 --> 36:43.656 align:middle
It is data dependent, and
we're-we're not locked

36:43.656 --> 36:46.336 align:middle
into any particular policy path.

36:46.796 --> 36:49.246 align:middle
Our-you know, as you said,

36:49.676 --> 36:52.066 align:middle
the data have not
notably strengthened.

36:52.286 --> 36:56.446 align:middle
I-there is noise always in the
data from quarter to quarter,

36:56.746 --> 36:59.086 align:middle
but we haven't changed
our view of the outlook.

36:59.086 --> 37:01.656 align:middle
We think we're on the same path,

37:01.656 --> 37:04.136 align:middle
not-we haven't boosted
the outlook,

37:04.206 --> 37:06.756 align:middle
projected faster growth.

37:07.416 --> 37:10.756 align:middle
We think we're moving along
the same course we've been on,

37:11.006 --> 37:14.186 align:middle
but it is one that
involves gradual tightening

37:14.186 --> 37:15.396 align:middle
in the labor market.

37:15.746 --> 37:19.756 align:middle
I would describe some
measures of wage growth

37:19.756 --> 37:21.526 align:middle
as having moved up some.

37:21.626 --> 37:25.146 align:middle
Some measures haven't moved
up, but there's some evidence

37:25.146 --> 37:29.036 align:middle
that wage growth is
gradually moving up,

37:29.526 --> 37:33.176 align:middle
which is also suggestive of
a strengthening labor market.

37:33.456 --> 37:38.036 align:middle
And we expect policy to remain
accommodative now for some time.

37:38.036 --> 37:41.456 align:middle
So we're-we're talking
about a gradual path

37:41.836 --> 37:44.356 align:middle
of removing policy accommodation

37:44.656 --> 37:49.126 align:middle
as the economy makes progress
moving toward neutral.

37:49.126 --> 37:52.386 align:middle
But we're continuing to
provide accommodation

37:52.616 --> 37:55.126 align:middle
to the economy that's
allowing it to grow

37:55.416 --> 37:57.966 align:middle
at an above-trend
pace that's consistent

37:58.226 --> 38:00.396 align:middle
with further improvement
in the labor market.

38:00.926 --> 38:05.666 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

38:05.876 --> 38:07.536 align:middle
Hi, John Heltman with
the American Banker.

38:07.566 --> 38:09.936 align:middle
A regulatory question, if I may.

38:10.356 --> 38:13.796 align:middle
The Administration has
recently reiterated its support

38:13.856 --> 38:15.816 align:middle
for a reinstatement
of Glass-Steagall.

38:16.176 --> 38:19.866 align:middle
Treasury Secretary
Mnuchin has called for a

38:19.866 --> 38:21.566 align:middle
"21st century Glass-Steagall."

38:21.896 --> 38:26.226 align:middle
Keeping in mind that there are
no specifics on this proposal,

38:26.636 --> 38:29.596 align:middle
is the fundamental idea of
separating commercial banking

38:29.596 --> 38:33.376 align:middle
from investment banking a
fruitful line of inquiry?

38:33.746 --> 38:36.096 align:middle
Is this the right
path to be pursuing?

38:38.036 --> 38:38.666 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:38.726 --> 38:42.936 align:middle
So I've not seen any concrete
proposals along this line.

38:42.936 --> 38:44.576 align:middle
I don't really know what a

38:44.576 --> 38:49.176 align:middle
"21st century Glass-Steagall"
would look like.

38:49.516 --> 38:54.506 align:middle
I think my reading on
the financial crisis is

38:54.646 --> 38:59.266 align:middle
that that wasn't the major
source of the financial crisis.

38:59.606 --> 39:03.896 align:middle
In fact, many of the
problems emanated from firms

39:03.896 --> 39:06.316 align:middle
that were investment
banking units.

39:06.376 --> 39:12.716 align:middle
To me, an important reform in
the aftermath of the crisis was

39:12.976 --> 39:18.426 align:middle
to make sure that investment
banking activities where-that

39:18.426 --> 39:22.136 align:middle
were a core part of the
shadow banking system

39:22.136 --> 39:27.186 align:middle
where leverage had built-that
those were appropriately

39:27.226 --> 39:30.276 align:middle
capitalized, had
appropriate liquidity,

39:30.276 --> 39:32.706 align:middle
and their management
was strengthened,

39:32.916 --> 39:35.666 align:middle
and that's what we
have tried to do.

39:35.666 --> 39:37.576 align:middle
But, obviously, we would look

39:37.576 --> 39:41.276 align:middle
at any proposals
that are put forward.

39:41.276 --> 39:44.076 align:middle
I'm not aware of anything
concrete to react to.

39:44.076 --> 39:44.466 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

39:44.846 --> 39:49.746 align:middle
So-so you don't think it's
necessarily [inaudible] a really

39:49.746 --> 39:50.146 align:middle
good idea?

39:50.146 --> 39:50.496 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

39:50.496 --> 39:52.996 align:middle
Well, I don't think it was the
cause of the financial crisis,

39:52.996 --> 39:59.936 align:middle
and I do feel that we have
significantly strengthened

39:59.936 --> 40:03.886 align:middle
supervision of bank
holding companies

40:03.886 --> 40:08.326 align:middle
that incorporate investment
banking activities.

40:08.936 --> 40:09.886 align:middle
JO LING KENT.

40:11.646 --> 40:14.186 align:middle
Hi, Chair Yellen.

40:14.186 --> 40:15.896 align:middle
I'm Jo Ling Kent with NBC News.

40:15.896 --> 40:18.406 align:middle
I just want to know, what
message are you trying

40:18.406 --> 40:20.776 align:middle
to send consumers with
this particular rate hike?

40:20.776 --> 40:21.186 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

40:21.716 --> 40:22.996 align:middle
I think that's a great question.

40:22.996 --> 40:24.266 align:middle
I appreciate your asking it.

40:24.816 --> 40:29.086 align:middle
And the simple message is,
the economy is doing well.

40:29.936 --> 40:33.466 align:middle
We have confidence in the
robustness of the economy

40:33.466 --> 40:35.706 align:middle
and its resilience to shocks.

40:36.446 --> 40:39.396 align:middle
It's performed well over
the last several years.

40:39.396 --> 40:43.756 align:middle
We have created, since
the trough in employment

40:43.756 --> 40:47.846 align:middle
after the financial crisis,
around 16 million jobs.

40:48.156 --> 40:51.446 align:middle
The unemployment rate
has moved way down,

40:51.796 --> 40:55.806 align:middle
and many more people
feel optimistic

40:55.806 --> 40:58.596 align:middle
about their prospects
in the labor market.

40:58.596 --> 41:00.416 align:middle
There is job security.

41:00.716 --> 41:06.106 align:middle
We're seeing more people who are
feeling free to quit their jobs,

41:06.106 --> 41:09.906 align:middle
getting outside offers,
looking for other opportunities.

41:10.376 --> 41:14.526 align:middle
So I think the job market, which
is an important focus for us,

41:14.526 --> 41:15.946 align:middle
is certainly improving.

41:16.196 --> 41:20.706 align:middle
That's not to say that it's
a-good labor market conditions

41:21.006 --> 41:23.876 align:middle
for every individual
in the United States.

41:23.976 --> 41:28.296 align:middle
We know there are problems
that face, particularly,

41:28.296 --> 41:32.646 align:middle
people with less skill and
education and in certain sectors

41:32.646 --> 41:37.496 align:middle
of the economy, but many
Americans are enjoying a

41:37.496 --> 41:42.256 align:middle
stronger labor market and feel
better-feel very much better

41:42.256 --> 41:42.876 align:middle
about that.

41:43.196 --> 41:46.386 align:middle
And inflation is
moving-moving up, I think,

41:46.386 --> 41:48.266 align:middle
toward our 2 percent objective.

41:48.536 --> 41:53.666 align:middle
And we're operating
in an environment

41:53.666 --> 41:56.736 align:middle
where the U.S. economy
is performing well

41:56.736 --> 41:58.956 align:middle
and risks seem pretty balanced.

41:58.956 --> 42:02.786 align:middle
So I think people can feel good
about the economic outlook.

42:03.686 --> 42:04.536 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

42:06.896 --> 42:08.716 align:middle
Hi, Jeanna Smialek,
Bloomberg News.

42:08.716 --> 42:10.556 align:middle
You emphasized in the statement

42:10.556 --> 42:13.256 align:middle
that the Fed's inflation
target is "symmetric."

42:13.416 --> 42:17.356 align:middle
Would you be able to expand a
little bit on why you did that,

42:17.356 --> 42:19.066 align:middle
why it was included, how much

42:19.066 --> 42:20.896 align:middle
of an overshoot would
the Fed be willing

42:20.896 --> 42:22.446 align:middle
to tolerate, and for how long?

42:22.846 --> 42:23.246 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

42:23.246 --> 42:27.816 align:middle
Well, a couple of years ago, we
included the word "symmetric"

42:27.816 --> 42:33.426 align:middle
in our statement of longer-run
goals, and this seemed

42:33.466 --> 42:36.346 align:middle
like an appropriate time
to introduce that word

42:36.346 --> 42:41.136 align:middle
into the statement because
we had previously indicated

42:41.136 --> 42:43.366 align:middle
that there was a
shortfall of inflation

42:43.646 --> 42:45.446 align:middle
from our 2 percent objective.

42:45.786 --> 42:49.006 align:middle
Now, headline inflation
has moved almost back

42:49.006 --> 42:50.116 align:middle
up to 2 percent.

42:50.306 --> 42:53.656 align:middle
As I indicated, a better
forward-looking measure

42:53.656 --> 42:57.616 align:middle
of inflation, core
inflation-that's not our target,

42:57.616 --> 42:59.276 align:middle
but I think it's worth looking

42:59.276 --> 43:02.646 align:middle
at because it's a better
forward-looking predictor

43:02.646 --> 43:06.766 align:middle
of headline inflation-I think
that's still running a little

43:06.766 --> 43:07.976 align:middle
bit under 2 percent,

43:08.026 --> 43:10.826 align:middle
but we expect it's
going to move up to 2.

43:11.216 --> 43:15.096 align:middle
And this seemed like a good
time to remind Americans

43:15.096 --> 43:18.986 align:middle
that what our objective
is is 2 percent inflation.

43:19.496 --> 43:23.636 align:middle
Inflation is not always
going to be at 2 percent.

43:23.726 --> 43:27.046 align:middle
It's-like all economic
variables, it fluctuates.

43:27.086 --> 43:30.666 align:middle
Sometimes it's going to be below
2 percent, sometimes it's going

43:30.666 --> 43:32.036 align:middle
to be above 2 percent.

43:32.306 --> 43:35.356 align:middle
We've had a long period
in which inflation has run

43:35.356 --> 43:36.426 align:middle
under 2 percent.

43:36.756 --> 43:41.096 align:middle
As we move back to 2 percent,
which is where we're heading,

43:41.346 --> 43:44.716 align:middle
there will be some times when
it's above 2 percent as well.

43:44.996 --> 43:48.536 align:middle
And it's a reminder-2
percent is not a ceiling

43:48.536 --> 43:50.106 align:middle
on inflation, it's a target.

43:50.106 --> 43:53.156 align:middle
It's where we always want
inflation to be heading.

43:53.156 --> 43:55.826 align:middle
And there will be some
times when inflation is

43:55.826 --> 43:59.116 align:middle
above 2 percent, just like
it's been below 2 percent.

43:59.336 --> 44:02.316 align:middle
We're not shooting for
inflation above 2 percent,

44:02.526 --> 44:06.456 align:middle
but it's a reminder that
there will be deviations above

44:06.456 --> 44:09.846 align:middle
and below when we're
achieving our objective.

44:09.846 --> 44:10.536 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

44:10.916 --> 44:13.056 align:middle
How well can you
tolerate an overshoot?

44:13.526 --> 44:14.276 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

44:14.276 --> 44:17.696 align:middle
Well, we would-if there were
an overshoot and it appeared

44:17.736 --> 44:21.696 align:middle
to be persistent, we would
put in place policies to try

44:21.696 --> 44:25.106 align:middle
to bring inflation
back to 2 percent.

44:25.536 --> 44:29.496 align:middle
That's the core set of
principles that we have adopted

44:29.496 --> 44:32.966 align:middle
in our statement on
longer-run goals and strategy.

44:33.266 --> 44:35.966 align:middle
And exactly how long it
would take to get back

44:35.966 --> 44:39.776 align:middle
to 2 percent would depend, in
part, on what was happening

44:39.776 --> 44:43.206 align:middle
with respect to employment
and our other objectives.

44:43.346 --> 44:46.016 align:middle
So there's no hard and
fast answer to that.

44:46.326 --> 44:49.396 align:middle
PATRICK GILLESPIE.

44:49.486 --> 44:51.266 align:middle
Chair Yellen, Patrick
Gillespie with CNN.

44:51.266 --> 44:53.216 align:middle
Some fiscal policy proposals,

44:53.216 --> 44:56.456 align:middle
such as the border adjustment
tax, would cause the dollar

44:56.456 --> 44:58.496 align:middle
to strengthen significantly
over a short period of time.

44:58.496 --> 45:01.466 align:middle
I realize you can't comment on
the specific policy proposal,

45:01.836 --> 45:04.486 align:middle
but if the dollar were
to strengthen quickly

45:04.546 --> 45:06.836 align:middle
over a short period of time,
perhaps some 20 percent

45:06.836 --> 45:07.786 align:middle
for whatever reason,

45:08.146 --> 45:10.226 align:middle
what would-what do you
think the impact would be

45:10.226 --> 45:11.836 align:middle
on the U.S. economy,
particularly

45:11.836 --> 45:13.776 align:middle
in exports and manufacturing?

45:14.066 --> 45:16.266 align:middle
And what do you think
would be any implications

45:16.266 --> 45:17.346 align:middle
for U.S. monetary policy?

45:17.886 --> 45:19.256 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

45:20.056 --> 45:23.106 align:middle
So that's a-that's a
difficult question to answer.

45:23.106 --> 45:28.276 align:middle
You asked that question
not in an isolated way:

45:28.276 --> 45:32.526 align:middle
What would the impact of a large
appreciation of the dollar be?

45:32.926 --> 45:37.146 align:middle
But, as I understand it, you
asked about it in the context

45:37.146 --> 45:38.736 align:middle
of a border tax adjustment.

45:38.736 --> 45:39.616 align:middle
Is that right?

45:39.946 --> 45:44.036 align:middle
Because the argument
that is made is

45:44.296 --> 45:46.256 align:middle
that what a border tax would do

45:46.256 --> 45:50.396 align:middle
without an exchange rate
adjustment is to raise the price

45:50.396 --> 45:53.096 align:middle
of imported goods into
the United States.

45:53.096 --> 45:56.986 align:middle
And that large movement
in the dollar

45:57.176 --> 46:02.876 align:middle
that analysts claim would occur
would essentially-if it were

46:02.876 --> 46:06.806 align:middle
complete, would fully
offset the impact

46:07.056 --> 46:10.326 align:middle
of the border tax on
U.S. import goods.

46:10.556 --> 46:14.066 align:middle
So, it wouldn't end
up having an impact

46:14.066 --> 46:18.466 align:middle
on U.S. inflation or GDP growth.

46:18.576 --> 46:22.956 align:middle
But a question that's a
very different-that's a very

46:22.956 --> 46:27.056 align:middle
different matter than if,
suddenly, for some reason,

46:27.476 --> 46:32.726 align:middle
the dollar were simply to begin
appreciating by a large amount,

46:32.726 --> 46:35.786 align:middle
say, because there were
flight-to-safety flows

46:35.786 --> 46:36.646 align:middle
into the dollar.

46:36.916 --> 46:39.556 align:middle
I mean, if-if the
latter were to occur,

46:39.556 --> 46:43.376 align:middle
there were just a big boost
in the dollar, it would tend

46:43.376 --> 46:45.626 align:middle
to put downward pressure
on inflation

46:45.996 --> 46:52.096 align:middle
and would have a negative
effect on U.S. export growth

46:52.096 --> 46:53.796 align:middle
and tend to boost imports.

46:53.876 --> 46:56.786 align:middle
But that's a, that's
a different-that's a

46:56.786 --> 46:57.766 align:middle
different exercise.

46:57.996 --> 47:02.246 align:middle
I would just say, it's very
uncertain exactly what would

47:02.286 --> 47:03.376 align:middle
happen to the dollar.

47:03.376 --> 47:06.096 align:middle
There has been a lot
of discussion of that,

47:06.096 --> 47:09.036 align:middle
and I think it's
complicated and uncertain.

47:09.386 --> 47:10.756 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

47:11.836 --> 47:12.566 align:middle
Hi, Chair Yellen.

47:13.256 --> 47:14.646 align:middle
Victoria Guida with Politico.

47:14.946 --> 47:17.456 align:middle
My question is about Republicans

47:17.456 --> 47:20.976 align:middle
on the House Financial Services
Committee wrote to you asking

47:20.976 --> 47:24.506 align:middle
that the Fed not put forward any
regulations until a Vice Chair

47:24.506 --> 47:26.556 align:middle
of Supervision is in place.

47:26.556 --> 47:28.576 align:middle
So I have a couple of
questions about that.

47:28.846 --> 47:33.796 align:middle
One is, are you all
pulling back at all

47:33.796 --> 47:37.586 align:middle
on any regulations-maybe not
all regulations, but, you know,

47:37.586 --> 47:40.206 align:middle
maybe only going forward
with ones that you see

47:40.206 --> 47:41.776 align:middle
as more time sensitive?

47:42.346 --> 47:45.126 align:middle
And during the hearing
before that committee,

47:45.126 --> 47:49.676 align:middle
you mentioned specifically the
CCAR stress-test rule would have

47:49.716 --> 47:52.166 align:middle
to come out sometime
before that next cycle.

47:52.626 --> 47:55.846 align:middle
Are there any other
time-sensitive regulations?

47:55.846 --> 47:56.566 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

47:56.656 --> 47:58.926 align:middle
So at this point
we don't have a lot

47:58.926 --> 48:01.046 align:middle
of time-sensitive regulations.

48:01.046 --> 48:08.266 align:middle
There is nothing right
now that we need to get

48:08.266 --> 48:10.386 align:middle
out that's a significant rule.

48:10.986 --> 48:16.516 align:middle
So we have a relatively light
regulatory agenda at this point.

48:16.896 --> 48:22.356 align:middle
We recognize, of course,
that we do have an obligation

48:22.356 --> 48:27.016 align:middle
to write the rules that
Congress, you know,

48:27.016 --> 48:29.636 align:middle
dictates in laws that they pass,

48:29.636 --> 48:33.986 align:middle
and so that is an ongoing
obligation-obligation

48:33.986 --> 48:34.756 align:middle
that we have.

48:34.856 --> 48:38.066 align:middle
But our calendar is
relatively light at this point.

48:39.006 --> 48:40.806 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

48:41.886 --> 48:43.956 align:middle
I'm Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

48:43.986 --> 48:44.396 align:middle
Back here.

48:44.496 --> 48:44.826 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:44.826 --> 48:46.446 align:middle
Yes. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

48:46.446 --> 48:50.456 align:middle
Some Fed critics have said it's
too soon to raise interest rates

48:50.496 --> 48:53.066 align:middle
because wages haven't
risen enough

48:53.066 --> 48:55.196 align:middle
to justify a rate increase.

48:55.196 --> 48:56.066 align:middle
What would you say to that?

48:56.066 --> 48:56.306 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:56.876 --> 49:00.876 align:middle
Well, I don't-I would
like to see wages increase

49:01.416 --> 49:03.986 align:middle
and think there's
some scope for them

49:03.986 --> 49:05.696 align:middle
to increase somewhat further.

49:06.106 --> 49:10.256 align:middle
But our objectives are maximum
employment and inflation,

49:10.656 --> 49:15.226 align:middle
and we need to consider what
path of rates is appropriate

49:15.226 --> 49:17.456 align:middle
to foster those objectives.

49:17.886 --> 49:21.516 align:middle
Unfortunately, one of the
things that's been holding

49:21.516 --> 49:25.556 align:middle
down wage increases is very
slow productivity growth.

49:26.006 --> 49:29.456 align:middle
And I think we are seeing
some upward pressure

49:29.456 --> 49:31.176 align:middle
as the labor market tightens.

49:31.506 --> 49:35.076 align:middle
I take that as a signal
that we're coming closer

49:35.076 --> 49:37.806 align:middle
to our maximum employment
objectives.

49:38.146 --> 49:42.726 align:middle
But productivity is-for those
focusing on wage growth,

49:43.016 --> 49:45.876 align:middle
productivity is an
additional important factor.

